OAI_PAPER({"slug": "Exact-Fourier-certificates-for-complex-Hadamard-matrices-of-order-six-September-24-2026", "title": "Exact Fourier certificates for complex Hadamard matrices of order six", "pdf": "https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/Exact-Fourier-certificates-for-complex-Hadamard-matrices-of-order-six-September-24-2026/Exact-Fourier-certificates-for-complex-Hadamard-matrices-of-order-six-September-24-2026.pdf", "family": "266", "family_title": "Exactly three mutually unbiased bases in dimension six", "abstract_html": "We prove the Fourier-vanishing conjecture of Matolcsi, Ruzsa, and Weiner: every complex Hadamard matrix of order six outside Tao's cubic equivalence class has a vanishing character sum at every permutation of \\((1,1,1,-1,-1,-1)\\). We also give an exact certificate excluding seven mutually unbiased bases in ℂ<sup>6</sup>; by Weiner's completion theorem, this yields an upper bound of five. Both certificates use only integer and rational arithmetic, and the complete verifier is included.", "blocks": [{"label": "Theorem 1", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "If \\(H\\) is not equivalent to \\(T\\), then \\( g_H(P_p\\alpha)=0 \\qquad\\text{for every coordinate permutation }p. \\)", "statement": "<p>**Fourier vanishing — original Theorem 1.1.**</p><p>Definitions and assumptions:</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\mathbb T=\\{z\\in\\mathbb C:|z|=1\\}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">A complex Hadamard matrix of order six is \\(H\\in\\mathbb T^{6\\times6}\\) satisfying \\(H^*H=6I\\).</p><p class=\"li\">A charge is \\(a\\in\\mathbb Z^6\\) satisfying \\(\\sum_{i=0}^5a_i=0\\). Define\n  \\[\n  g_H(a)=\\frac16\\sum_{k=0}^5\\prod_{i=0}^5H(i,k)^{a_i}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n  \\[\n  \\alpha=(1,1,1,-1,-1,-1),\\qquad \\omega=e^{2\\pi i/3}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Define \\(T=(\\omega^{t_{ij}})_{i,j=0}^5\\) by\n  \\[\n  t_{ij}=\n  \\begin{cases}\n  0,&amp;i=0\\text{ or }j=0\\text{ or }i=j,\\\\\n  1,&amp;i,j\\in\\{1,\\ldots,5\\},\\ i-j\\equiv\\pm1\\pmod5,\\\\\n  -1,&amp;\\text{otherwise}.\n  \\end{cases}\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Two Hadamard matrices are equivalent if they differ by row and column permutations and multiplication of individual rows and columns by elements of \\(\\mathbb T\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For a coordinate permutation \\(p\\), write\n  \\[\n  (P_pa)_i=a_{p(i)}.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>If \\(H\\) is not equivalent to \\(T\\), then\n\\[\ng_H(P_p\\alpha)=0\n\\qquad\\text{for every coordinate permutation }p.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Suppose \\(g_H(P_p\\alpha)\\ne0\\) for some coordinate permutation \\(p\\). This is equivalent to failure of condition (M) in Proposition 2, since row permutation of the matrix permutes the charge.</p><p>Proposition 2 therefore implies that \\(H^{\\circ2}\\) is Hadamard. Proposition 3 makes its dephased entries cube roots of unity, and Proposition 4 identifies the resulting matrix with \\(T\\).</p><p>Thus \\(H\\) is equivalent to \\(T\\), contradicting the assumption.</p><p>The vanishing assertion respects equivalence: balanced charges make the character sums invariant under column phases; row phases multiply each character by a unit scalar; and row and column permutations preserve the family of asserted vanishings.</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 2", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There do not exist seven pairwise mutually unbiased orthonormal bases in \\(\\mathbb C^6\\).", "statement": "<p>**No complete family — original Theorem 1.2.**</p><p>Definitions and assumptions:</p><p class=\"li\">The Hermitian inner product is conjugate-linear in its first argument.</p><p class=\"li\">Orthonormal bases \\(B_r=(b_{r,0},\\ldots,b_{r,5})\\) and \\(B_s=(b_{s,0},\\ldots,b_{s,5})\\) of \\(\\mathbb C^6\\) are mutually unbiased if\n  \\[\n  |\\langle b_{r,i},b_{s,j}\\rangle|^2=\\frac16\n  \\qquad(0\\le i,j\\le5).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(N(6)\\) denote the maximum number of pairwise mutually unbiased orthonormal bases in \\(\\mathbb C^6\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There do not exist seven pairwise mutually unbiased orthonormal bases in \\(\\mathbb C^6\\).</p><p>Consequently, by Weiner’s completion theorem, which states that any \\(d\\) mutually unbiased bases in \\(\\mathbb C^d\\) extend to a complete family,\n\\[\nN(6)\\le5.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Suppose seven pairwise mutually unbiased bases exist.</p><p>Apply Proposition 2 to every transition Hadamard matrix. Proposition 6 excludes condition (C) for every transition, so all transitions satisfy condition (M):\n\\[\ng_{H_{rs}}(P\\alpha)=0\n\\qquad(r\\ne s,\\ P\\text{ a coordinate permutation}).\n\\]\nThus Proposition 7 applies.</p><p>For a complex vector \\(Y=x+iy\\) and a real symmetric positive definite matrix \\(Z\\),\n\\[\nY^*ZY=x^{\\mathsf T}Zx+y^{\\mathsf T}Zy\\ge0.\n\\]\nHence\n\\[\n5\\sum_\\lambda10^{15-e_\\lambda}\n\\mathbb E(Y_\\lambda^*Z_\\lambda Y_\\lambda)\\ge0.\n\\]\nProposition 7 gives a strictly negative upper bound for the same quantity, a contradiction.</p><p>Therefore seven mutually unbiased bases do not exist.</p><p>Weiner’s completion theorem states that six mutually unbiased bases in \\(\\mathbb C^6\\) would extend to seven. Hence six do not exist either, and\n\\[\nN(6)\\le5.\n\\]</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 3", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "By multiplying rows and columns by unit complex numbers and permuting rows and columns, one can transform \\(H\\) into \\(T\\).", "statement": "<p>**Classification of the cubic alternative — original Proposition 4.1.**</p><p>Definitions and assumptions:</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(H\\in\\mathbb T^{6\\times6}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Write \\(H^{\\circ k}\\) for its entrywise \\(k\\)-th power.</p><p class=\"li\">Assume that both \\(H\\) and \\(H^{\\circ2}\\) are complex Hadamard matrices.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(T\\) and equivalence be as defined in Theorem 1.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>By multiplying rows and columns by unit complex numbers and permuting rows and columns, one can transform \\(H\\) into \\(T\\).</p><p>In particular, every entry of the transformed matrix is a cube root of unity.</p>", "proof": "<p>Dephase \\(H\\) by multiplying rows and columns by unit complex numbers. These operations preserve both Hadamard conditions: on \\(H^{\\circ2}\\) they induce the corresponding squared phase factors.</p><p>Proposition 3 shows that every entry of the dephased matrix is a cube root of unity. Proposition 4 then transforms it into \\(T\\) by row and column permutations.</p><p>Therefore the original \\(H\\) is equivalent to \\(T\\), as asserted.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 1", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Every real vector \\(\\mathbf m\\in\\mathbb R^N\\) satisfying \\(W\\mathbf m=0\\) obeys \\( \\mathbf m=P\\mathbf m. \\) Thus the rows of \\(P\\) give valid substitutions for all moment coordinates in terms of the coordinates indexed …", "statement": "<p>**Exact certification of linear substitutions — original Lemma 7.1.**</p><p>Definitions and assumptions:</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(W\\in\\mathbb Z^{r\\times N}\\) consist of selected homogeneous moment equations after denominators have been cleared. The constant moment is included as one coordinate.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(J\\subseteq\\{1,\\ldots,N\\}\\) have cardinality \\(r\\), and suppose that the square submatrix \\(W_J\\) is invertible modulo a prime.</p><p class=\"li\">Put \\(F=\\{1,\\ldots,N\\}\\setminus J\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(P\\in\\mathbb Q^{N\\times N}\\) satisfy\n  \\[\n  P_{F,F}=I,\\qquad P_{*,J}=0,\\qquad WP=0.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Every real vector \\(\\mathbf m\\in\\mathbb R^N\\) satisfying \\(W\\mathbf m=0\\) obeys\n\\[\n\\mathbf m=P\\mathbf m.\n\\]\nThus the rows of \\(P\\) give valid substitutions for all moment coordinates in terms of the coordinates indexed by \\(F\\). No rationality assumption on the moment values is required.</p>", "proof": "<p>Invertibility modulo the prime implies that the integer \\(\\det W_J\\) is nonzero. In the coordinate order \\(J,F\\), write\n\\[\nW=(W_J\\ W_F),\\qquad\nP=\n\\begin{pmatrix}\n0&amp;P_{J,F}\\\\\n0&amp;I\n\\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\nThe exact equality \\(WP=0\\) gives\n\\[\nP_{J,F}=-W_J^{-1}W_F.\n\\]\nEvery solution of \\(W\\mathbf m=0\\) consequently satisfies\n\\[\n\\mathbf m_J=P_{J,F}\\mathbf m_F,\n\\]\nwhile the free coordinates of \\(P\\mathbf m\\) are already \\(\\mathbf m_F\\). Hence \\(\\mathbf m=P\\mathbf m\\).</p><p>The two certificates below use the following implementation of this argument.</p><p class=\"li num\">1. Clear denominators in the generated equations.</p><p class=\"li num\">2. Perform modular elimination with prime \\(1000000007\\), retaining an original integer equation whenever it supplies a new pivot. These retained equations form \\(W\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">3. Choose each pivot at the largest remaining coordinate. Back substitution processes pivot coordinates in ascending order, so the final rule for a pivot contains only free coordinates.</p><p class=\"li num\">4. Repeat on the retained equations using the further moduli\n   \\[\n   1000000009,\\ 998244353,\\ 1000003,\\ 2013265921,\\ 754974721,\\\n   32452843,\\ 86028121,\n   \\]\n   retaining matching pivot sets, and combine coefficients by the Chinese remainder algorithm.</p><p class=\"li num\">5. Use rational reconstruction to propose rational rules. Give every nonpivot coordinate its identity rule.</p><p class=\"li num\">6. For every retained integer equation, substitute the proposed rules, clear all denominators, and check that every integer coefficient is zero.</p><p>The last step is exactly the verification \\(WP=0\\). Modular elimination proves that \\(W_J\\) is invertible modulo the first prime. The construction gives \\(P_{F,F}=I\\) and \\(P_{*,J}=0\\). Therefore the substitutions hold for every actual real moment vector satisfying the selected equations.</p><p>No assertion is made that the modular pivot count equals the full rational rank of all generated equations. Additional true equations may remain unused.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 2", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "At least one of the following holds:", "statement": "<p>**Single-matrix dichotomy — original Corollary 3.1.**</p><p>Definitions and assumptions:</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(H\\) be a complex Hadamard matrix of order six.</p><p class=\"li\">For a row permutation \\(\\pi\\), define\n  \\[\n  H_\\pi(i,k)=H(\\pi(i),k).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">For a balanced integer vector \\(a\\), define\n  \\[\n  \\operatorname{ord}(a)=\\sum_{a_i&gt;0}a_i.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">An exponent matrix is an integer matrix \\(A=(A_{uv})\\) whose row sums and column sums are zero.</p><p class=\"li\">If \\(A\\) has \\(p\\le6\\) rows and \\(q\\le6\\) columns, define\n  \\[\n  m_H(A)=\n  \\mathbb E\\operatorname{Re}\n  \\prod_{u=0}^{p-1}\\prod_{v=0}^{q-1}\n  K(\\rho(u),\\kappa(v))^{A_{uv}},\n  \\]\n  where:</p><p class=\"li l2\">\\(K\\) is \\(H\\) or \\(H^{\\mathsf T}\\), each with probability \\(1/2\\);</p><p class=\"li l2\">\\(\\rho:\\{0,\\ldots,p-1\\}\\to\\{0,\\ldots,5\\}\\) and\n    \\(\\kappa:\\{0,\\ldots,q-1\\}\\to\\{0,\\ldots,5\\}\\) are independent uniform injections;</p><p class=\"li l2\">all three choices are independent.</p><p class=\"li\">Set \\(m_H(\\varnothing)=1\\). Then \\(|m_H(A)|\\le1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For a function \\(F\\) of a Hadamard matrix, define\n  \\[\n  \\langle F(\\widetilde H)\\rangle_H\n  =\n  \\frac1{2\\cdot6!}\n  \\sum_{K\\in(H,H^{\\mathsf T})}\\sum_{\\pi\\in S_6}F(K_\\pi).\n  \\]\n  The ordered pair \\((H,H^{\\mathsf T})\\) is counted with multiplicity.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>At least one of the following holds:\n\\[\n\\textnormal{(M)}\\qquad\ng_{H_\\pi}(\\alpha)=0\n\\quad\\text{for every row permutation }\\pi;\n\\]\n\\[\n\\textnormal{(C)}\\qquad\nH^{\\circ2}\\text{ is a complex Hadamard matrix}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The average in Lemma 2.3 is finite, every orientation and row permutation has positive weight, and every summand is nonnegative. Consequently, for every row ordering,\n\\[\ng_{H_\\pi}(\\alpha)g_{H_\\pi}(q_{\\mathrm{cross}})=0,\n\\qquad\ng_{H_\\pi}(\\alpha)g_{H_\\pi}(q_{\\mathrm{same}})=0.\n\\]</p><p>Suppose (M) fails. A witnessing row ordering divides the row indices into triples \\(I_+\\) and \\(I_-\\) on which the charge is \\(+1\\) and \\(-1\\), respectively. Permutations within either triple leave this nonzero character unchanged.</p><p>For any \\(i\\in I_+\\) and \\(j\\in I_-\\), place them in positions \\(0\\) and \\(3\\). The first identity gives\n\\[\ng_H(2(e_i-e_j))=0.\n\\]\nFor distinct indices of \\(I_+\\), place them in positions \\(0\\) and \\(1\\). The second identity gives the same vanishing.</p><p>Interchanging the two triples conjugates the nonzero character. The same argument therefore treats pairs within \\(I_-\\) and all remaining ordered cross pairs. Hence\n\\[\ng_H(2(e_i-e_j))=0\n\\qquad(i\\ne j).\n\\]\nTherefore\n\\[\n\\sum_{k=0}^5H(i,k)^2\\overline{H(j,k)^2}\n=\n6g_H(2(e_i-e_j))=0\n\\qquad(i\\ne j).\n\\]\nEvery row of \\(H^{\\circ2}\\) has squared norm six, so\n\\[\nH^{\\circ2}(H^{\\circ2})^*=6I.\n\\]\nFor a square matrix this also implies\n\\[\n(H^{\\circ2})^*H^{\\circ2}=6I.\n\\]\nThus (C) holds.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "If \\(p\\le6\\), then \\( (7-p)m_H(A) = -\\sum_{\\substack{0\\le\\ell<p\\\\\\ell\\ne i}} m_H(A_{i\\to\\ell}). \\) For a formal matrix with \\(p=7\\), the valid relation is …", "statement": "<p>**Contraction of an order-one row — original Lemma 3.2.**</p><p>Definitions and assumptions:</p><p class=\"li\">Use the injective moments in Proposition 2.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(A\\) be a balanced exponent matrix with \\(p\\) rows and \\(q\\le6\\) columns.</p><p class=\"li\">Suppose row \\(i\\) has order one.</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(\\ell\\ne i\\), let \\(A_{i\\to\\ell}\\) be obtained by adding row \\(i\\) to row \\(\\ell\\) and deleting row \\(i\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>If \\(p\\le6\\), then\n\\[\n(7-p)m_H(A)\n=\n-\\sum_{\\substack{0\\le\\ell&lt;p\\\\\\ell\\ne i}}\nm_H(A_{i\\to\\ell}).\n\\tag{5}\n\\]</p><p>For a formal matrix with \\(p=7\\), the valid relation is\n\\[\n\\sum_{\\substack{0\\le\\ell&lt;7\\\\\\ell\\ne i}}\nm_H(A_{i\\to\\ell})=0.\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\nEvery moment in this relation has at most six rows; no seven-row injective moment is defined or used.</p><p>The analogous relations hold for columns.</p><p>The moments are unchanged by row permutations, column permutations, global negation, transposition, and deletion of zero rows or columns.</p>", "proof": "<p>Permuting the rows or columns of \\(A\\) does not change the injection averages. Replacing \\(A\\) by \\(-A\\) conjugates the monomial and leaves its real part unchanged. Transposition exchanges the injections and interchanges the equally weighted choices \\(H,H^{\\mathsf T}\\).</p><p>A zero row or column contributes a factor one. Deleting a coordinate of a uniformly chosen injection leaves a uniformly chosen injection on the remaining coordinates, so such a row or column can be deleted.</p><p>Since row \\(i\\) is balanced and has order one, it has a \\(+1\\) in one column \\(v_+\\), a \\(-1\\) in another column \\(v_-\\), and zeros elsewhere. Fix \\(K\\), the column injection \\(\\kappa\\), and an injective assignment of the other \\(p-1\\) rows. Let \\(M\\) be the product of the factors contributed by those rows. Column orthogonality gives\n\\[\nM\\sum_{t=0}^5\nK(t,\\kappa(v_+))\\overline{K(t,\\kappa(v_-))}=0.\n\\]\nBoth choices of \\(K\\) are Hadamard matrices because, for a square matrix, column orthogonality also gives row orthogonality.</p><p>When \\(p\\le6\\), there are \\(7-p\\) unused row labels. Summing over them gives \\(7-p\\) times the conditional expectation obtained by assigning row \\(i\\) uniformly to an unused label. Averaging produces \\((7-p)m_H(A)\\).</p><p>For each occupied label, that of row \\(\\ell\\), the corresponding monomial adds the exponents of row \\(i\\) to those of row \\(\\ell\\). Its average is \\(m_H(A_{i\\to\\ell})\\). Taking real parts and averaging proves (5).</p><p>When \\(p=7\\), the six other rows occupy all available row labels. There is no unused-label contribution, and the same orthogonality equation gives (6). Transposition gives the column versions.</p><p>Repeated contraction expresses every moment with both dimensions at most six as a rational linear combination of the empty moment and moments whose nonzero rows and columns all have order at least two. Each contraction decreases the number of rows or columns, so the procedure terminates.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "\\( \\left\\langle \\operatorname{Re}\\prod_{j=1}^ng_{\\widetilde H}(a_j) \\right\\rangle_H = \\sum_{\\substack{\\mathcal S\\text{ a set partition of }\\{1,\\ldots,n\\}\\\\ |\\mathcal S|\\le6}} \\frac{(6)_{|\\mathcal S|}}{6^n}\\, m_H(A_{\\math …", "statement": "<p>**Equality-pattern expansion — original Lemma 3.3.**</p><p>Definitions and assumptions:</p><p class=\"li\">Use the setting of Proposition 2.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(a_1,\\ldots,a_n\\in\\mathbb Z^6\\) be balanced charges satisfying\n  \\[\n  \\sum_{j=1}^na_j=0.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(A\\) have columns \\(a_1,\\ldots,a_n\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For a set partition \\(\\mathcal S\\) of \\(\\{1,\\ldots,n\\}\\), let \\(A_{\\mathcal S}\\) have one column\n  \\[\n  \\sum_{j\\in S}a_j\n  \\]\n  for each block \\(S\\in\\mathcal S\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Write\n  \\[\n  (6)_k=6\\cdot5\\cdots(6-k+1),\\qquad (6)_0=1.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n\\left\\langle\n\\operatorname{Re}\\prod_{j=1}^ng_{\\widetilde H}(a_j)\n\\right\\rangle_H\n=\n\\sum_{\\substack{\\mathcal S\\text{ a set partition of }\\{1,\\ldots,n\\}\\\\\n|\\mathcal S|\\le6}}\n\\frac{(6)_{|\\mathcal S|}}{6^n}\\,\nm_H(A_{\\mathcal S}).\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\nThe ordering of the blocks is immaterial.</p>", "proof": "<p>Expanding the product samples \\(n\\) columns independently and uniformly from the six columns of \\(\\widetilde H\\). Group the summands by their equality pattern.</p><p>For a specified partition with \\(k\\) blocks, the distinct block labels have \\((6)_k\\) assignments, each of probability \\(6^{-n}\\). Conditional on this pattern, the block labels form a uniform injection. Multiplying factors with the same column label adds their exponents, giving \\(A_{\\mathcal S}\\).</p><p>Averaging over the row permutation, orientation, and real part gives its injective moment. Partitions with more than six blocks have no assignments.</p><p>Every resulting exponent matrix is balanced because the individual charges and their total sum are balanced. Also,\n\\[\ng_H(-a)=\\overline{g_H(a)},\n\\]\nso the expansion applies to absolute squares of products.</p><p>A zero charge may be omitted because \\(g_H(0)=1\\). For example,\n\\[\n\\left\\langle |g_{\\widetilde H}(a)|^2\\right\\rangle_H\n=\n\\frac16+\\frac56m_H([a,-a]).\n\\]</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.3", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For each \\(q\\in\\{q_{\\mathrm{cross}},q_{\\mathrm{same}}\\}\\), \\( 3\\left\\langle |L_{\\widetilde H}|^2\\right\\rangle_H + \\left\\langle |g_{\\widetilde H}(\\alpha)g_{\\widetilde H}(q)|^2 \\right\\rangle_H =0. \\)", "statement": "<p>**Single-matrix certificate — original Proposition 3.4.**</p><p>Definitions and assumptions:</p><p class=\"li\">Use the setting of Proposition 2.</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n  \\[\n  w=(-3,-2,0,1,2,2),\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  \\begin{aligned}\n  a_1&amp;=(-1,-1,0,0,1,1),&amp;\n  a_2&amp;=(-2,0,0,0,1,1),\\\\\n  a_3&amp;=(-1,-1,0,1,0,1),&amp;\n  a_4&amp;=(-1,-1,0,1,1,0).\n  \\end{aligned}\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n  \\[\n  L_H=-g_H(w)+\\sum_{j=1}^4c_jg_H(a_j)g_H(w-a_j),\n  \\qquad\n  (c_1,c_2,c_3,c_4)=(2,1,1,1).\n  \\tag{9}\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n  \\[\n  q_{\\mathrm{cross}}=(2,0,0,-2,0,0),\n  \\qquad\n  q_{\\mathrm{same}}=(2,-2,0,0,0,0).\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For each \\(q\\in\\{q_{\\mathrm{cross}},q_{\\mathrm{same}}\\}\\),\n\\[\n3\\left\\langle |L_{\\widetilde H}|^2\\right\\rangle_H\n+\n\\left\\langle\n|g_{\\widetilde H}(\\alpha)g_{\\widetilde H}(q)|^2\n\\right\\rangle_H\n=0.\n\\tag{10}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Expand the two absolute squares and apply Lemma 2.2 to every resulting product. Each product has total charge zero, at most four factors, and sum of charge orders ten. Thus the expression is a rational linear combination of injective moments.</p><p>Apply Lemma 2.1 until every remaining nonzero row and column has order at least two. Select representatives under row and column permutations, global negation, and transposition.</p><p>The remaining reduction uses the following finite family of true seven-row equations.</p><p class=\"li num\">1. Choose total weight\n   \\[\n   k\\in\\{7,8,9,10,11\\},\n   \\qquad\n   \\operatorname{wt}(A)=\\sum_i\\operatorname{ord}(A_{i,*}),\n   \\]\n   and a column count\n   \\[\n   2\\le q\\le\\min(4,\\lfloor k/2\\rfloor).\n   \\]</p><p class=\"li num\">2. Choose nondecreasing row-order and column-order sequences\n   \\[\n   (r_0,\\ldots,r_6),\\qquad(c_0,\\ldots,c_{q-1}),\n   \\]\n   each summing to \\(k\\), with\n   \\[\n   1\\le r_i\\le\\lfloor k/2\\rfloor,\\qquad\n   2\\le c_j\\le\\lfloor k/2\\rfloor.\n   \\]</p><p class=\"li num\">3. Enumerate balanced integer \\(7\\times q\\) arrays with those orders. Rows of equal order may be lexicographically ordered, and duplicate canonical representatives may be skipped.</p><p class=\"li num\">4. The first row has order one: seven positive row orders summing to at most eleven cannot all be at least two.</p><p class=\"li num\">5. For each retained array use\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{\\ell=1}^6 U_0(A_{0\\to\\ell})=0,\n   \\tag{44}\n   \\]\n   where \\(U_0\\) is the recursive contraction expansion from Lemma 2.1.</p><p>All evaluated arrays have at most six rows and six columns. Only the formal source arrays have seven rows.</p><p>The verifier’s canonicalization removes zero axes, considers\n\\[\nA,\\ -A,\\ A^{\\mathsf T},\\ -A^{\\mathsf T},\n\\]\nretains orientations with at least as many rows as columns, orders columns by their positive-part orders, and minimizes over the remaining column permutations after lexicographically sorting rows. These operations only select names for equal moments.</p><p>Rows are enumerated as differences of disjointly supported nonnegative compositions of the prescribed order. Remaining positive and negative column capacities enforce the column orders.</p><p>The finite pair table has\n\\[\n8871\\text{ nonzero equations},\\qquad\n909\\text{ coordinates},\\qquad\n897\\text{ selected witness rows}.\n\\]\nThe coordinate order is first sorted by \\((\\operatorname{wt}(A),A)\\), then reordered using the initial priority list\n\\[\n[0,1,2,5,4,6,7,8,9,55,80,228].\n\\]\nProposition 1 certifies the resulting exact substitutions.</p><p>Let \\(U(A)\\) be the contraction expansion followed by these substitutions. For charges \\(a_1,\\ldots,a_n\\), \\(n\\le4\\), the final product expansion is\n\\[\n\\sum_{\\substack{\\mathcal S\\text{ a set partition}\\\\|\\mathcal S|\\le6}}\n\\frac{(6)_{|\\mathcal S|}}{6^n}\\,U(A_{\\mathcal S}).\n\\tag{45}\n\\]</p><p>The routine `check_pair` in Appendix A forms \\(L_H\\) with coefficients\n\\[\n-1,2,1,1,1.\n\\]\nIt multiplies each monomial by the conjugate of every monomial and expands \\(3|L_H|^2\\) by (45). For each specified \\(q\\), it adds the expansion with charge columns\n\\[\n(\\alpha,q,-\\alpha,-q).\n\\]\nAfter the certified substitutions, every coefficient is zero for both choices of \\(q\\).</p><p>Thus (10) is an exact identity for every actual moment vector. Its verification uses only the contractions, moment symmetries, equality-pattern expansion, and exact integer checks printed in Appendix A.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 3", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "After dephasing \\(H\\), every entry belongs to \\( \\{1,\\omega,\\omega^2\\}. \\)", "statement": "<p>**Cube-root rigidity for the cubic alternative — first part of original Proposition 4.1.**</p><p>Definitions and assumptions:</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(H\\) and \\(H^{\\circ2}\\) be complex Hadamard matrices of order six.</p><p class=\"li\">A matrix is dephased if its initial row and initial column consist of ones.</p><p class=\"li\">Put \\(\\omega=e^{2\\pi i/3}\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>After dephasing \\(H\\), every entry belongs to\n\\[\n\\{1,\\omega,\\omega^2\\}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>By Lemma 3.2, it remains to exclude (11).</p><p>Restrict each row of \\(H\\) to the three columns of the \\(xJ_3\\) block. For rows from opposite classes, the ratio cubes to \\(x\\) or \\(x^{-1}\\) on these columns and to \\(1\\) on the other three.</p><p>The two rotated triples in Lemma 3.1 are therefore separated by this division of the columns. Their sums are zero, so restrictions of rows from opposite classes are mutually orthogonal.</p><p>Each class spans a space of dimension at least two on these three columns. Indeed, suppose the three restrictions in one class span a line. Each has squared norm three, so the inner product of any two has modulus three. Orthogonality of the full rows forces their complementary restrictions to have inner product of modulus three as well. Equality in Cauchy–Schwarz makes those complementary restrictions proportional.</p><p>The three full rows would then lie in a space of dimension at most two, contrary to their orthogonality.</p><p>Thus the two mutually orthogonal restricted spans have dimensions at least two, which is impossible in \\(\\mathbb C^3\\). This excludes (11). Hence \\(H^{\\circ3}\\) is all ones after dephasing.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The multiset of coordinates of \\(v\\) is the union of two rotated triples of cube roots of unity.", "statement": "<p>**Row-ratio and column-ratio structure.**</p><p>Definitions and assumptions:</p><p class=\"li\">Assume the setting of Proposition 3.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(v=(v_0,\\ldots,v_5)\\) be the entrywise ratio of two distinct rows of \\(H\\), or of two distinct columns of \\(H\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The multiset of coordinates of \\(v\\) is the union of two rotated triples of cube roots of unity. The two triples may coincide.</p><p>Consequently, the cubed coordinates of \\(v\\) have either one value six times or two distinct values three times each.</p>", "proof": "<p>Set\n\\[\np_k(v)=\\sum_{i=0}^5v_i^k.\n\\]\nThe two Hadamard conditions give\n\\[\np_1(v)=p_2(v)=0.\n\\]\nIf \\(e_k\\) is the \\(k\\)-th elementary symmetric function of the six coordinates, Newton’s identities give\n\\[\ne_1=e_2=0.\n\\]\nSince \\(|v_i|=1\\),\n\\[\ne_{6-k}=e_6\\overline{e_k},\n\\]\nso \\(e_4=e_5=0\\) as well. Consequently,\n\\[\n\\prod_{i=0}^5(z-v_i)=z^6-e_3z^3+e_6.\n\\]\nFactoring this as a quadratic in \\(z^3\\) gives the claimed union of two rotated cube-root triples.</p><p>Both \\(H\\) and \\(H^{\\circ2}\\) also have orthogonal columns, so the same argument applies to column ratios.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "All nonconstant rows of \\(H^{\\circ3}\\) have the same support, and the analogous assertion holds for columns.", "statement": "<p>**Possible nonconstant cube pattern.**</p><p>Definitions and assumptions:</p><p class=\"li\">Assume the setting of Proposition 3.</p><p class=\"li\">Dephase \\(H\\), which preserves both Hadamard conditions.</p><p class=\"li\">A nonconstant row of \\(H^{\\circ3}\\) has the value \\(1\\) three times and a value \\(x\\ne1\\) three times; call the three positions carrying \\(x\\) its support.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>All nonconstant rows of \\(H^{\\circ3}\\) have the same support, and the analogous assertion holds for columns.</p><p>If \\(H^{\\circ3}\\) is not all ones, then after arranging the row and column classes,\n\\[\nH^{\\circ3}=\n\\begin{pmatrix}\nJ_3&amp;J_3\\\\\nJ_3&amp;xJ_3\n\\end{pmatrix},\n\\qquad x\\ne1,\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\nwhere \\(J_3\\) is the all-ones matrix of order three.</p>", "proof": "<p>By Lemma 3.1 and dephasing, each row of \\(H^{\\circ3}\\) is either all ones or has the stated form. Its nonconstant support excludes column zero.</p><p>Suppose two distinct supports \\(S,U\\) have respective values \\(x,y\\ne1\\), and put \\(k=|S\\cap U|\\). They are three-element subsets of five positions, so \\(k\\in\\{1,2\\}\\). Their cubed row ratio has values and multiplicities\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|cccc}\n\\text{value}&amp;1&amp;x&amp;y^{-1}&amp;xy^{-1}\\\\ \\hline\n\\text{multiplicity}&amp;k&amp;3-k&amp;3-k&amp;k.\n\\end{array}\n\\]\nAll multiplicities are positive, but Lemma 3.1 permits at most two distinct cubed values.</p><p>The three values \\(1,x,y^{-1}\\) therefore force \\(x=y^{-1}\\). Next \\(x^2\\) must equal \\(1\\) or \\(x\\), forcing \\(x=y=-1\\). The multiplicities would then be \\(2k\\) and \\(6-2k\\), namely two and four in some order, contradicting the required multiplicities three and three.</p><p>Thus all nonconstant rows have the same support. Applying the same argument to columns proves the column assertion.</p><p>If \\(H^{\\circ3}\\) is not all ones, its common nonconstant-row support consists of three columns. Each such column has exactly three non-one entries, so exactly three rows are nonconstant. In any one of these columns, those three non-one entries coincide. Hence their row values agree, giving (11).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 4", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Permuting the noninitial rows and columns transforms \\(H\\) into \\(T\\).", "statement": "<p>**Classification of the dephased cube-root matrix — final part of original Proposition 4.1.**</p><p>Definitions and assumptions:</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(H\\) be a dephased complex Hadamard matrix of order six.</p><p class=\"li\">Assume every entry of \\(H\\) is a cube root of unity.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(T\\) be the matrix defined in Theorem 1.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Permuting the noninitial rows and columns transforms \\(H\\) into \\(T\\).</p><p>Moreover, \\(T\\) and \\(T^{\\circ2}\\) are Hadamard matrices.</p>", "proof": "<p>In every noninitial row and column, orthogonality to the initial one implies that each cube root occurs twice. Thus there is exactly one additional entry equal to one in each noninitial row and column.</p><p>These entries form a matching and may be placed on the diagonal by a column permutation. Write the remaining entries as\n\\[\nH(i,j)=\\omega^{s_{ij}},\n\\qquad\ns_{ij}\\in\\{-1,1\\},\n\\qquad\ni,j\\in\\{1,\\ldots,5\\},\\ i\\ne j.\n\\]\nEach such row has two plus signs and two minus signs.</p><p>For two distinct noninitial rows \\(i,j\\), their ratio contains each cube root twice. They agree at column zero and disagree at columns \\(i,j\\). Thus they agree at exactly one of the remaining three columns.</p><p>There are two sign mismatches among those three columns, giving\n\\[\n\\prod_{k\\notin\\{0,i,j\\}}s_{ik}s_{jk}=1.\n\\]\nThe product of the four signs in each complete noninitial row is also one. It follows that\n\\[\ns_{ij}s_{ji}=1,\n\\]\nso \\(s_{ij}=s_{ji}\\).</p><p>The plus signs define a simple undirected graph of degree two on five vertices. Its components are cycles of length at least three, so it is a single five-cycle. A simultaneous permutation of the noninitial rows and columns gives \\(T\\).</p><p>For completeness, every difference of distinct exponent rows of \\(T\\) contains each residue modulo three twice. This is checked by separating an initial-row pair, an adjacent pair on the five-cycle, and a nonadjacent pair. Hence \\(T\\) is Hadamard. Its entrywise square is \\(\\overline T\\), which is Hadamard as well.</p><p>The paper identifies \\(T\\) with the usual spectral matrix \\(S_6\\) by\n\\[\nT_{ij}=(S_6)_{p(i),p(j)},\n\\qquad\n(p(0),\\ldots,p(5))=(0,1,2,5,4,3).\n\\]</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 5", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For every external basis \\(s\\ne r\\), \\( g_s(d)=0\\qquad(d\\in D). \\) For every order-two charge \\(a\\), \\( \\sum_{s\\ne r}g_s(a)=0. \\) For arbitrary charges \\(a,b\\) and each coordinate \\(i\\) …", "statement": "<p>**Character identities of a complete family — original Lemma 5.1.**</p><p>Definitions and assumptions:</p><p class=\"li\">Suppose \\(B_1,\\ldots,B_7\\) are pairwise mutually unbiased orthonormal bases in \\(\\mathbb C^6\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(r\\ne s\\), define\n  \\[\n  H_{rs}(i,k)=\\sqrt6\\,\\langle b_{r,i},b_{s,k}\\rangle.\n  \\]\n  Then \\(H_{rs}\\) is Hadamard and \\(H_{sr}=H_{rs}^*\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Fix a reference basis \\(r\\), and abbreviate \\(H_s=H_{rs}\\) and \\(g_s=g_{H_s}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let\n  \\[\n  D=\\{e_i-e_j:0\\le i,j\\le5,\\ i\\ne j\\}.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every external basis \\(s\\ne r\\),\n\\[\ng_s(d)=0\\qquad(d\\in D).\n\\]\nFor every order-two charge \\(a\\),\n\\[\n\\sum_{s\\ne r}g_s(a)=0.\n\\tag{12}\n\\]</p><p>For arbitrary charges \\(a,b\\) and each coordinate \\(i\\),\n\\[\n\\sum_{j=0}^5\ng_s(a+e_i-e_j)g_s(b-e_i+e_j)\n=\ng_s(a+b).\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\nThe same identity holds with both shifts reversed.</p><p>For distinct external bases \\(s,t\\ne r\\),\n\\[\n\\sum_{d\\in D}g_s(a+d)g_t(b-d)=0.\n\\tag{14}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The order-one vanishing is row orthogonality.</p><p>For the order-two identity, put\n\\[\nP_{s,k}=b_{s,k}b_{s,k}^*,\n\\]\nand let \\(\\mathcal D_s^0\\) be the complex space of traceless matrices diagonal in \\(B_s\\). If \\(s\\ne t\\), then\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Tr}\\bigl((P_{s,k}-I/6)(P_{t,l}-I/6)\\bigr)=0.\n\\]\nThus the spaces \\(\\mathcal D_s^0\\) are pairwise orthogonal for the Hilbert–Schmidt inner product. Each has dimension five, and their dimensions sum to\n\\[\n35=6^2-1.\n\\]\nOrthogonal projection gives the complex bilinear identity\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Tr}(XY)\n=\n\\sum_{s=1}^7\\sum_{k=0}^5\n\\operatorname{Tr}(XP_{s,k})\\operatorname{Tr}(YP_{s,k})\n\\qquad\n(\\operatorname{Tr}X=\\operatorname{Tr}Y=0).\n\\tag{15}\n\\]\nIt follows by pairing the complex-linear projection formula for \\(X\\) with \\(Y\\).</p><p>Every order-two charge is \\(d_1+d_2\\) for two members of \\(D\\). In the reference basis choose off-diagonal matrix units \\(X,Y\\) whose traces against an external projector are\n\\[\n\\frac{h_k^{d_1}}6,\\qquad \\frac{h_k^{d_2}}6,\n\\]\nwhere \\(h_k\\) is column \\(k\\) of \\(H_s\\). For example, \\(d_1=e_i-e_j\\) corresponds to \\(X=E_{ji}\\).</p><p>Since \\(d_1+d_2\\) has order two, \\(d_2\\ne-d_1\\), so \\(\\operatorname{Tr}(XY)=0\\). The reference basis contributes zero to (15), while external basis \\(s\\) contributes \\(g_s(d_1+d_2)/6\\). This proves (12).</p><p>For (13), expand the character sums and let \\(x,y\\) be the sampled columns. The left side is\n\\[\n\\frac1{36}\\sum_{x,y}x^ay^b\\frac{x_i}{y_i}\n\\sum_{j=0}^5\\frac{y_j}{x_j}.\n\\]\nThe inner sum is six when \\(x=y\\) and zero otherwise, by column orthogonality. The result is \\(g_s(a+b)\\). Conjugating the inner orthogonality relation gives the reversed-shift version.</p><p>Finally, if \\(x\\) is a column of \\(H_s\\) and \\(y\\) a column of \\(H_t\\), with \\(s\\ne t\\), then\n\\[\n\\left|\\sum_{i=0}^5\\frac{x_i}{y_i}\\right|^2=6.\n\\]\nTherefore\n\\[\n\\sum_{d\\in D}(x/y)^d\n=\n\\left|\\sum_{i=0}^5\\frac{x_i}{y_i}\\right|^2-6=0.\n\\]\nExpanding the products of character sums proves (14).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 6", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "No transition matrix \\(H_{rs}\\) can have \\(H_{rs}^{\\circ2}\\) Hadamard.", "statement": "<p>**Cubic-pair obstruction — original Proposition 5.2.**</p><p>Definitions and assumptions:</p><p class=\"li\">Suppose \\(B_1,\\ldots,B_7\\) are pairwise mutually unbiased orthonormal bases in \\(\\mathbb C^6\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Their transition Hadamard matrices are\n  \\[\n  H_{rs}(i,k)=\\sqrt6\\,\\langle b_{r,i},b_{s,k}\\rangle\n  \\qquad(r\\ne s).\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>No transition matrix \\(H_{rs}\\) can have \\(H_{rs}^{\\circ2}\\) Hadamard.</p>", "proof": "<p>Suppose a transition \\(H\\) has \\(H^{\\circ2}\\) Hadamard. Propositions 3 and 4 allow rephasing and reordering the corresponding basis vectors so that the transition is \\(T\\). These operations preserve mutual unbiasedness.</p><p>Use the first basis of this pair as reference. Let \\(K_1,\\ldots,K_5\\) be its other five transition matrices, and put\n\\[\nf(b)=\\frac15\\sum_{s=1}^5g_{K_s}(b),\n\\qquad\nG(b)=g_T(b).\n\\]\nProposition 5 gives order-one vanishing and\n\\[\nf(b)=-\\frac15G(b)\n\\qquad(\\operatorname{ord}(b)=2).\n\\tag{16}\n\\]\nApplying its cross identity with second charge \\(-c\\), and averaging over the five \\(K_s\\), gives\n\\[\n\\sum_{d\\in D}f(a+d)\\overline{G(c+d)}=0\n\\tag{17}\n\\]\nfor all balanced \\(a,c\\).</p><p>Define\n\\[\nS(b)=\\frac1{6!}\\sum_{\\pi\\in S_6}\n\\operatorname{Re}\\bigl(f(P_\\pi b)\\overline{G(P_\\pi b)}\\bigr).\n\\tag{18}\n\\]\nSince both \\(f\\) and \\(G\\) conjugate under negation, this equals the average over the signed permutation orbit.</p><p>Every entry of \\(T\\) has cube one, so \\(G(3d)=1\\) for \\(d\\in D\\). Let \\(z\\) be a uniform column of a uniformly chosen matrix among \\(K_1,\\ldots,K_5\\). Then \\(\\mathbb Ez^b=f(b)\\), and\n\\[\n0\\le\n\\mathbb E\\left|\\sum_{i=0}^5z_i^3\\right|^2\n=\n6+\\sum_{d\\in D}f(3d)\n=\n6+30S(3(e_5-e_0)).\n\\tag{19}\n\\]</p><p>Apply (17) to every \\((A,C)\\in\\mathcal O\\), multiply by \\(\\overline{G(A-C)}\\), sum, and take real parts. With \\(Q,R\\) from Lemma 6.2, conjugacy of \\(f\\) permits symmetrizing the coefficients, giving\n\\[\n\\sum_b\\operatorname{Re}\\bigl(f(b)\\overline{R(b)}\\bigr)=0.\n\\tag{21}\n\\]</p><p>Put \\(S_j=S(w_j)\\). The coefficients and orbit sizes in Lemma 6.2 give\n\\[\n180S_3+360S_6+180S_7+360S_8=0,\n\\]\nhence\n\\[\nS_3+2S_6+S_7+2S_8=0.\n\\tag{24}\n\\]\nThe table and Lemma 6.1 give\n\\[\nS_1=S_4=S_5=S_9=0.\n\\]\nAt types \\(6\\) and \\(8\\), \\(|G|^2=1/4\\). Equation (16) therefore gives\n\\[\nS_6=S_8=-\\frac1{20}.\n\\tag{25}\n\\]</p><p>Next take \\(a=c=w_5\\) in (17) and average over coordinate permutations. Repeat with \\(a=c=w_8\\). The signed permutation types of the thirty charges \\(w_j+d\\), \\(d\\in D\\), have counts\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|rrrrrrrr}\n&amp;0&amp;1&amp;3&amp;4&amp;6&amp;7&amp;8&amp;9\\\\ \\hline\nw_5+D&amp;1&amp;8&amp;12&amp;0&amp;8&amp;0&amp;0&amp;1\\\\\nw_8+D&amp;0&amp;0&amp;4&amp;8&amp;4&amp;2&amp;8&amp;4.\n\\end{array}\n\\]\nThese counts are obtained by adding each \\(e_i-e_j\\) and sorting coordinates, allowing a simultaneous sign change.</p><p>Substituting the known values gives\n\\[\nS_0+12S_3=\\frac25,\n\\qquad\n4S_3+2S_7=\\frac35.\n\\tag{26}\n\\]\nTogether with (24) and (25), these imply\n\\[\nS_3=S_7=\\frac1{10},\n\\qquad\nS_0=-\\frac45.\n\\]\nSince \\(w_0=3(e_5-e_0)\\), equation (19) becomes\n\\[\n\\mathbb E\\left|\\sum_{i=0}^5z_i^3\\right|^2\n=\n6+30S_0=-18,\n\\]\ncontradicting nonnegativity.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Every permutation of the rows of \\(T\\) can be completed to a symmetry of \\(T\\) by column permutations, row and column phase factors, and, if necessary, complex conjugation.", "statement": "<p>**Symmetries of the cubic matrix — original Lemma 5.3.**</p><p>Definitions and assumptions:</p><p class=\"li\">For this lemma, no complete family is assumed.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(T\\) be the fixed cubic matrix from Theorem 1, and put\n  \\[\n  G(b)=g_T(b)\n  \\]\n  for balanced charges \\(b\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Every permutation of the rows of \\(T\\) can be completed to a symmetry of \\(T\\) by column permutations, row and column phase factors, and, if necessary, complex conjugation.</p><p>Consequently, \\(|G(b)|\\) is constant on every orbit of balanced charges under coordinate permutations and negation.</p>", "proof": "<p>Normalize the phase factor of output row zero to one. The all-one output row and column then determine all other phase factors, removing the common inverse row-column phase freedom.</p><p>Choose an initial row and column in \\(36\\) ways, choose whether to conjugate, and dephase at the chosen row and column. Propositions 3 and 4 apply again.</p><p>After matching the additional ones on the diagonal, exactly ten orders of the remaining rows, together with matching column orders, make the plus graph the specified five-cycle: choose its initial vertex and direction. This gives\n\\[\n36\\cdot2\\cdot10=720\n\\]\ndistinct normalized symmetry operations. Their permutations recover the chosen initial row and column; together with conjugation, their remaining orders recover the cycle isomorphism.</p><p>It remains to show that their row permutations are distinct. Work with exponent columns\n\\[\nt_j=(t_{ij})_{i=0}^5\n\\]\nmodulo three, and set \\(X=\\{t_0,\\ldots,t_5\\}\\).</p><p>A symmetry fixing the row order acts as\n\\[\nX\\longmapsto sX+u,\\qquad s\\in\\{1,-1\\}.\n\\]\nBecause row zero has zero exponents, the column phases are common and can be absorbed into the row phases. The image of the zero column shows that \\(u\\in X\\).</p><p>For \\(j\\ne0\\), column \\(t_j\\) has exactly one zero in its noninitial positions, at position \\(j\\). Hence \\(-t_j\\notin X\\): its zero would force equality with \\(t_j\\), which is false.</p><p>A nonzero translation preserving \\(X\\) would contain both \\(u\\) and \\(2u=-u\\), and is impossible. Negation with \\(u=0\\) is impossible for the same reason.</p><p>Suppose \\(s=-1\\) and \\(u=t_x\\ne0\\). Choose a neighbor \\(y\\) of \\(x\\) on the five-cycle, and let \\(z\\) be their common non-neighbor. The vector \\(t_x-t_y\\) has its unique noninitial zero at \\(z\\). At the other two positions outside \\(\\{x,y,z\\}\\), exactly one of \\(x,y\\) is adjacent to that vertex, so the differences are nonzero modulo three.</p><p>If \\(t_x-t_y\\) were a column, it would therefore equal \\(t_z\\). But at positions \\(x,y\\), the former has entries \\(-1,1\\), whereas \\(t_z\\) has entries \\(-1,-1\\), a contradiction.</p><p>Thus the only normalized symmetry fixing the row order is the identity. Two of the 720 operations with the same row permutation would have such a quotient. Their row permutations are therefore distinct and exhaust \\(S_6\\).</p><p>For balanced charges, column phases cancel, row phases multiply the character by a unit scalar, and conjugation conjugates it. This proves the orbit assertion, including negation because \\(G(-b)=\\overline{G(b)}\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The following table lists representatives of every balanced charge of order at most three, up to permutation and negation.", "statement": "<p>**Exact coefficients of the cubic cross identity — original Sections 5.4–5.5 and Table 1.**</p><p>Definitions and assumptions:</p><p class=\"li\">No complete family is assumed in this coefficient calculation.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(G(b)=g_T(b)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n  \\[\n  a=(-1,-1,0,0,1,1),\\qquad\n  c=(0,0,0,0,-1,1),\n  \\]\n  and let \\(\\mathcal O\\) be their set of distinct simultaneous coordinate permutations.</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n  \\[\n  Q(b)=\n  \\sum_{\\substack{(A,C)\\in\\mathcal O\\\\b-A\\in D}}\n  G(A-C)G(b-A+C).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(z\\in\\mathbb C\\), write \\(z^{[1]}=z\\) and \\(z^{[-1]}=\\overline z\\), and define\n  \\[\n  R(b)=\\frac12\n  \\sum_{\\substack{(A,C)\\in\\mathcal O,\\ \\epsilon\\in\\{-1,1\\}\\\\\n  \\epsilon b-A\\in D}}\n  \\bigl[G(A-C)G(\\epsilon b-A+C)\\bigr]^{[\\epsilon]}.\n  \\tag{20}\n  \\]\n  Equivalently,\n  \\[\n  R(b)=\\frac{Q(b)+\\overline{Q(-b)}}2.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The following table lists representatives of every balanced charge of order at most three, up to permutation and negation. Its integer triples satisfy\n\\[\n6G(w_j)=\\sum_{m=0}^2N_m\\omega^m,\n\\qquad\n72R(w_j)=\\sum_{m=0}^2U_m\\omega^m.\n\\tag{22}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|c|c|c}\nj&amp;w_j&amp;(N_0,N_1,N_2)&amp;(U_0,U_1,U_2)\\\\ \\hline\n0&amp;(-3,0,0,0,0,3)&amp;(6,0,0)&amp;(0,0,0)\\\\\n1&amp;(-3,0,0,0,1,2)&amp;(2,2,2)&amp;(0,0,0)\\\\\n2&amp;(-3,0,0,1,1,1)&amp;(3,0,3)&amp;(0,0,0)\\\\\n3&amp;(-2,-1,0,0,1,2)&amp;(4,1,1)&amp;(60,42,42)\\\\\n4&amp;(-2,-1,0,1,1,1)&amp;(2,2,2)&amp;(144,144,144)\\\\\n5&amp;(-2,0,0,0,0,2)&amp;(2,2,2)&amp;(0,0,0)\\\\\n6&amp;(-2,0,0,0,1,1)&amp;(3,3,0)&amp;(180,180,72)\\\\\n7&amp;(-1,-1,-1,1,1,1)&amp;(3,3,0)&amp;(540,540,216)\\\\\n8&amp;(-1,-1,0,0,1,1)&amp;(4,1,1)&amp;(480,336,336)\\\\\n9&amp;(-1,0,0,0,0,1)&amp;(2,2,2)&amp;(576,576,576)\\\\\n10&amp;(0,0,0,0,0,0)&amp;(6,0,0)&amp;(0,0,0)\n\\end{array}\n\\]</p><p>On the signed permutation orbit of \\(w_j\\),\n\\[\nR(b)=\\lambda_jG(b),\n\\qquad\n\\lambda_3=\\frac12,\\quad\n\\lambda_6=3,\\quad\n\\lambda_7=9,\\quad\n\\lambda_8=4,\n\\tag{23}\n\\]\nand \\(R(b)=0\\) on all other orbits.</p><p>The signed permutation orbit sizes for \\(j=3,6,7,8\\) are\n\\[\n360,\\quad120,\\quad20,\\quad90,\n\\]\nrespectively.</p>", "proof": "<p>The \\(180\\) pairs in \\(\\mathcal O\\) are uniquely parametrized by\n\\[\nA=e_p+e_q-e_h-e_i,\\qquad\nC=e_q-e_p,\n\\]\nwhere \\(p\\ne q\\), \\(h&lt;i\\), and \\(\\{h,i\\}\\cap\\{p,q\\}=\\varnothing\\). Thus\n\\[\nu=A-C=2e_p-e_h-e_i.\n\\]</p><p>For a balanced charge \\(b\\), define\n\\[\nv(b,k)=\\sum_{\\ell=0}^5b_\\ell t_{\\ell k}\\pmod3,\n\\]\nwith residues in \\(\\{0,1,2\\}\\).</p><p>For each representative \\(b=w_j\\), initialize integer arrays \\(N,U\\) of length three to zero.</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(k=0,\\ldots,5\\), increment \\(N_{v(b,k)}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For each admissible \\(p,q,h,i\\), each \\(\\epsilon\\in\\{-1,1\\}\\) satisfying \\(\\epsilon b-A\\in D\\), and each \\(k,l\\in\\{0,\\ldots,5\\}\\), increment\n  \\[\n  U_{\\epsilon\\left(v(u,k)+v(\\epsilon b-u,l)\\right)\\bmod3}.\n  \\]</p><p>These are the finite loops printed after Table 1. Each product of two \\(G\\)-terms contributes \\(36\\) cube roots with coefficient \\(1/36\\); the factor \\(1/2\\) in \\(R\\) gives denominator \\(72\\). The loops give the displayed integer triples.</p><p>For example,\n\\[\nG(w_3)=\\frac{4+\\omega+\\omega^2}{6}=\\frac12,\n\\qquad\nR(w_3)=\\frac{60+42\\omega+42\\omega^2}{72}=\\frac14.\n\\]\nUsing \\(1+\\omega+\\omega^2=0\\) in each row gives the stated coefficients at the representatives.</p><p>To extend the calculation to their orbits, every factor in (20) has a balanced charge, so column phases cancel. The two charges in each product add to \\(\\epsilon b\\); after the prescribed conjugation, its row phase is exactly the row phase for \\(G(b)\\).</p><p>Row permutations preserve the full set \\(\\mathcal O\\), and conjugation conjugates both \\(R\\) and \\(G\\). Lemma 6.1 therefore gives the asserted covariance on every orbit. Also,\n\\[\nR(-b)=\\overline{R(b)}.\n\\]</p><p>No higher order can occur in the support of \\(R\\), because\n\\[\n\\epsilon b=A+d,\\qquad \\operatorname{ord}(A)=2,\\quad d\\in D\n\\]\nimplies \\(\\operatorname{ord}(b)\\le3\\). The orbit sizes are obtained by counting coordinate permutations, including negation.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 7", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Every \\(Z_\\lambda\\) is positive definite, and the mixed-moment identities imply …", "statement": "<p>**Mixed-moment certificate — original Proposition 6.3.**</p><p>Definitions and assumptions:</p><p class=\"li\">Suppose \\(B_1,\\ldots,B_7\\) are pairwise mutually unbiased orthonormal bases in \\(\\mathbb C^6\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Assume additionally that every transition matrix satisfies\n  \\[\n  g_{H_{rs}}(P\\alpha)=0\n  \\qquad(r\\ne s,\\ P\\text{ a coordinate permutation}).\n  \\tag{27}\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Choose a reference index \\(r\\) uniformly from the seven indices. Independently choose:</p><p class=\"li l2\">a uniform coordinate permutation \\(\\sigma\\in S_6\\);</p><p class=\"li l2\">a uniform bijection \\(\\pi:\\{0,\\ldots,5\\}\\to\\{1,\\ldots,7\\}\\setminus\\{r\\}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">In this sampling, write\n  \\[\n  H_s(i,k)=\\sqrt6\\,\\langle b_{r,\\sigma(i)},b_{\\pi(s),k}\\rangle,\n  \\qquad g_s=g_{H_s}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">For labels \\(\\mathbf s=(s_1,\\ldots,s_n)\\) and balanced charges\n  \\(\\mathbf a=(a_1,\\ldots,a_n)\\) satisfying \\(\\sum_ja_j=0\\), define\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal M(\\mathbf s;\\mathbf a)\n  =\n  \\mathbb E\\operatorname{Re}\\prod_{j=1}^ng_{s_j}(a_j).\n  \\tag{28}\n  \\]\n  Equal labels always denote the same matrix. The empty moment is one.</p><p class=\"li\">Define averaged pair moments\n  \\[\n  m(A)=\\frac1{42}\\sum_{r\\ne s}m_{H_{rs}}(A),\n  \\tag{29}\n  \\]\n  where \\(m_H\\) is the injective moment defined in Proposition 2.</p><p class=\"li\">Both kinds of actual moments satisfy\n  \\[\n  |m(A)|\\le1,\\qquad |\\mathcal M(\\mathbf s;\\mathbf a)|\\le1.\n  \\tag{30}\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Choose, in addition, a uniform column \\(z\\) of \\(H_0\\), shared by every function below. For a balanced charge \\(a\\), define\n  \\[\n  F_0(a)=g_0(a)z^{-a},\n  \\qquad\n  F_1(a)=\\left(\\frac15\\sum_{s=1}^5g_s(a)\\right)z^{-a}.\n  \\tag{31}\n  \\]</p><p>The fixed certificate data:</p><p class=\"li\">Use exactly the six literal records named `data` in Appendix A, Listing 1, lines \\(443\\)–\\(571\\) of the paper.</p><p class=\"li\">Each record is \\((\\lambda,\\mathrm{ws},e_\\lambda,\\mathbf a_\\lambda)\\), where\n  \\[\n  \\mathrm{ws}=((t_0,v_0),\\ldots,(t_{m-1},v_{m-1})),\n  \\qquad t_i\\in\\{0,1\\},\\quad v_i\\in\\mathbb Z^6,\\quad \\sum_j(v_i)_j=0.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Define the symmetric integer matrix \\(Z_\\lambda\\) by its listed lower triangle:\n  \\[\n  (Z_\\lambda)_{ij}=(Z_\\lambda)_{ji}\n  =\n  (\\mathbf a_\\lambda)_{i(i+1)/2+j}\n  \\qquad(0\\le j\\le i&lt;m).\n  \\tag{38}\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">The six shapes, sizes, and scale exponents are\n  \\[\n  \\begin{array}{c|r|r}\n  \\lambda&amp;m&amp;e_\\lambda\\\\ \\hline\n  (1,1,1,2)&amp;5&amp;14\\\\\n  (1,1,3)&amp;21&amp;13\\\\\n  (1,2,2)&amp;11&amp;13\\\\\n  (1,4)&amp;27&amp;14\\\\\n  (2,3)&amp;22&amp;14\\\\\n  (5)&amp;12&amp;15.\n  \\end{array}\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">For each \\(\\lambda\\), place \\(1,\\ldots,5\\), in order, into successive left-justified rows of lengths \\(\\lambda_1,\\lambda_2,\\ldots\\). Let \\(R_\\lambda\\) be the group of permutations within these rows and \\(C_\\lambda\\) the group of permutations within the resulting columns. Both fix position zero.</p><p class=\"li\">With \\((P_pv)_i=v_{p(i)}\\), define\n  \\[\n  (Y_\\lambda)_i\n  =\n  \\sum_{p\\in R_\\lambda}\\sum_{q\\in C_\\lambda}\n  \\operatorname{sgn}(q)\\,\n  F_{t_i}(P_pP_qv_i).\n  \\tag{39}\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Every \\(Z_\\lambda\\) is positive definite, and the mixed-moment identities imply\n\\[\n5\\sum_\\lambda10^{15-e_\\lambda}\n\\mathbb E(Y_\\lambda^*Z_\\lambda Y_\\lambda)\n\\le\n\\frac{-613302797399911+179721388988719}{6480}\n=\n-\\frac{2007321335237}{30}&lt;0.\n\\tag{40}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 7.4 proves that every \\(Z_\\lambda\\) is positive definite.</p><p>By Lemma 7.5,\n\\[\n\\mathcal Q=c_0+\\sum_{K\\ne\\varnothing}c_K\\mu_K.\n\\]\nEvery original moment satisfies \\(|\\mu_K|\\le1\\): pair moments average unit-modulus monomials, and mixed moments average products of character sums of absolute value at most one.</p><p>Consequently,\n\\[\n\\mathcal Q\n\\le\nc_0+\\sum_{K\\ne\\varnothing}|c_K|\n=\n\\frac{-613302797399911+179721388988719}{6480}\n=\n-\\frac{2007321335237}{30}&lt;0.\n\\]\nThis is the stated mixed-moment certificate.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For \\((s,t)\\in\\{0,1\\}^2\\setminus\\{(1,1)\\}\\), \\( \\operatorname{Re}\\mathbb E\\bigl(F_s(a)\\overline{F_t(v)}\\bigr) = \\mathcal M((s,t,0);\\mathbf b). \\) For the remaining case …", "statement": "<p>**Gram entries — original Lemma 6.1.**</p><p>Definitions and assumptions:</p><p class=\"li\">Assume the sampling and functions of Proposition 7.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(a,v\\) be balanced charges, and put\n  \\[\n  \\mathbf b=(a,-v,v-a).\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For \\((s,t)\\in\\{0,1\\}^2\\setminus\\{(1,1)\\}\\),\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Re}\\mathbb E\\bigl(F_s(a)\\overline{F_t(v)}\\bigr)\n=\n\\mathcal M((s,t,0);\\mathbf b).\n\\tag{32}\n\\]\nFor the remaining case,\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Re}\\mathbb E\\bigl(F_1(a)\\overline{F_1(v)}\\bigr)\n=\n\\frac15\\mathcal M((1,1,0);\\mathbf b)\n+\n\\frac45\\mathcal M((1,2,0);\\mathbf b).\n\\tag{33}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Condition on all the matrices and average over the shared column:\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_z z^{v-a}=g_0(v-a).\n\\]</p><p>For a term with exactly one \\(F_1\\), all five summands have the same expectation by label exchangeability.</p><p>For a term with two \\(F_1\\) factors, expand the two sums independently. Among the \\(25\\) ordered label choices from \\(\\{1,\\ldots,5\\}\\), five have equal labels and twenty have distinct labels. Their marginal distributions give the coefficients \\(1/5\\) and \\(4/5\\).</p><p>Thus averaging removes the auxiliary column completely. A zero charge contributes \\(g_s(0)=1\\) and may be deleted. Removing unused labels preserves the uniform injection law on the retained labels.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "A common coordinate permutation, simultaneous negation of all charges, simultaneous reordering of factors and labels, and arbitrary injective renaming of used labels preserve \\(\\mathcal M\\).", "statement": "<p>**Mixed-moment reductions — original Lemma 6.2.**</p><p>Definitions and assumptions:</p><p class=\"li\">Assume all hypotheses and definitions of Proposition 7, including (27).</p><p class=\"li\">A mixed moment has labels \\(\\mathbf s=(s_1,\\ldots,s_n)\\) and charge columns \\(\\mathbf a=(a_1,\\ldots,a_n)\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p class=\"li num\">1. A common coordinate permutation, simultaneous negation of all charges, simultaneous reordering of factors and labels, and arbitrary injective renaming of used labels preserve \\(\\mathcal M\\). Zero charge factors may be omitted. Coordinates on which every charge is zero may be omitted, with the remaining coordinates interpreted as a uniform injection into the six row positions.</p><p class=\"li num\">2. A mixed moment vanishes if any charge has order one or is a coordinate permutation of \\(\\alpha\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">3. Suppose \\(\\operatorname{ord}(a_j)=2\\), and label \\(s_j\\) occurs only once. Let \\(S\\) be the set of used labels and \\(\\ell=|S|\\). If \\(\\mathbf s_{j\\to t}\\) replaces \\(s_j\\) by \\(t\\), then\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal M(\\mathbf s;\\mathbf a)\n   =\n   -\\frac1{7-\\ell}\n   \\sum_{t\\in S\\setminus\\{s_j\\}}\n   \\mathcal M(\\mathbf s_{j\\to t};\\mathbf a).\n   \\tag{34}\n   \\]</p><p class=\"li num\">4. If all labels agree, let \\(A\\) have columns \\(a_1,\\ldots,a_n\\), after deletion of zero rows. For a set partition \\(\\mathcal P\\), let \\(A_{\\mathcal P}\\) have one summed charge column for each block. Then\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal M((s,\\ldots,s);\\mathbf a)\n   =\n   \\sum_{\\substack{\\mathcal P\\text{ a set partition}\\\\|\\mathcal P|\\le6}}\n   \\frac{(6)_{|\\mathcal P|}}{6^n}\\,m(A_{\\mathcal P}).\n   \\tag{35}\n   \\]</p><p class=\"li num\">5. For balanced \\(a,b,c\\) satisfying \\(a+b+c=0\\),\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{j=0}^5\n   \\mathcal M\\bigl((s,s,u);\n   (a+e_i-e_j,b-e_i+e_j,c)\\bigr)\n   =\n   \\mathcal M((s,u);(a+b,c)).\n   \\tag{36}\n   \\]\n   The same identity holds with both shifts reversed. Also, for \\(s\\ne t\\),\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{d\\in D}\n   \\mathcal M((s,t,u);(a+d,b-d,c))=0.\n   \\tag{37}\n   \\]\n   The label \\(u\\) may coincide with another label.</p>", "proof": "<p>Uniform sampling gives coordinate and label symmetries; commutativity gives factor reordering. Simultaneous negation conjugates the product and preserves its real part. A zero charge contributes one. Removing unused labels or coordinates leaves a uniform injection on those retained.</p><p>The vanishing assertions follow from Hadamard orthogonality and (27). A permuted \\(\\alpha\\) has six nonzero coordinates, so deleting zero coordinate rows does not alter whether a charge has this type.</p><p>For the singleton rule, fix the reference basis, row permutation, and bases assigned to the \\(\\ell-1\\) other used labels. There are \\(7-\\ell\\) available bases for the singleton label. The order-two identity in Proposition 5 gives\n\\[\n\\sum_{\\substack{v\\ne r\\\\v\\text{ not already assigned}}}\\widetilde g_v(a_j)\n=\n-\\sum_{t\\in S\\setminus\\{s_j\\}}\\widetilde g_t(a_j),\n\\]\nwith the same row order throughout. Multiply by the remaining factors and average. The marginal distribution of the retained labels is the distribution defining each moment on the right of (34).</p><p>For the single-label expansion, sample columns independently and group them by equality partition, as in Lemma 2.2. A partition with \\(k\\) blocks has \\((6)_k\\) distinct assignments among \\(6^n\\) choices.</p><p>To justify the transpose averaging in \\(m(A)\\), the ordered basis pair is uniform and\n\\[\nH_{sr}=\\overline{H_{rs}}^{\\mathsf T}.\n\\]\nExchanging the bases exchanges row and column injections and sends \\(A\\) to \\(-A^{\\mathsf T}\\). Global negation conjugates the monomial and leaves its real part unchanged. Thus the ordered-pair average is already transpose-invariant, proving (35).</p><p>Finally, multiply (13) or (14) from Proposition 5 by \\(g_u(c)\\), then take real expectations. This gives (36) and (37).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.3", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The finite generation and reductions specified below produce \\( 46041\\text{ nonzero equations},\\qquad 3429\\text{ moment coordinates},\\qquad 2767\\text{ selected witness rows}. \\) The exact substitutions certified from …", "statement": "<p>**Finite mixed table and exact substitutions — original Section 7.3.**</p><p>Definitions and assumptions:</p><p class=\"li\">Assume the setting of Proposition 7.</p><p class=\"li\">A mixed key consists of a label tuple \\(\\mathbf s\\) and a balanced exponent array \\(A\\) whose columns are its charges.</p><p class=\"li\">The separate tag \\((\\varnothing,A)\\) names the pair moment \\(m(A)\\); the completely empty key names the constant one.</p><p class=\"li\">Canonical mixed keys are selected by simultaneous factor and label permutations, renaming labels by first occurrence, sorting common coordinate rows, simultaneous negation, and deletion of zero factors and zero coordinate rows.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The finite generation and reductions specified below produce\n\\[\n46041\\text{ nonzero equations},\\qquad\n3429\\text{ moment coordinates},\\qquad\n2767\\text{ selected witness rows}.\n\\]\nThe exact substitutions certified from these witness rows fix every actual moment vector satisfying the hypotheses of Proposition 7.</p>", "proof": "<p>The routine `evmix` recursively expands keys as follows.</p><p class=\"li num\">1. A charge of order one or of permuted-\\(\\alpha\\) type makes the mixed moment zero.</p><p class=\"li num\">2. If at most one label remains, use the equality-pattern expansion and certified pair substitutions from Lemma 2.3, attaching the pair tag to every remaining coordinate.</p><p class=\"li num\">3. If such a single-label expression is zero by the preceding vanishing rule, record its pair expansion as a zero equation in `rows0`.</p><p class=\"li num\">4. Otherwise, if an order-two charge has a singleton label, use (34) and repeat recursively. Every replacement decreases the number of distinct labels.</p><p class=\"li num\">5. If no reduction applies, retain the key as an original mixed-moment coordinate.</p><p>These reductions are justified by Lemma 7.2. In particular, the pair expansion is valid for the ordered-pair average.</p><p>The finite triple enumeration `triples(11)` generates balanced charges \\(a,b,c\\in\\mathbb Z^6\\) satisfying\n\\[\na+b+c=0,\n\\qquad\n0\\le\\operatorname{ord}(c)\\le\\operatorname{ord}(b)\n\\le\\operatorname{ord}(a)\\le5,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\operatorname{ord}(a)+\\operatorname{ord}(b)+\\operatorname{ord}(c)\\le11.\n\\]\nChoose \\(a\\) up to coordinate permutation and sign by taking the lesser of its sorted entries and the sorted entries of \\(-a\\). Enumerate all balanced \\(b\\) of order at most \\(\\operatorname{ord}(a)\\), put \\(c=-a-b\\), and retain those satisfying the restrictions.</p><p>Balanced vectors of a given order are differences of disjointly supported nonnegative compositions. Sorting the six coordinate rows removes common-coordinate duplicates.</p><p>For each generated array:</p><p class=\"li\">Evaluate all five equality patterns on the three labels, registering single-label zero equations.</p><p class=\"li\">Consider distinct permutations of the three charge columns, retaining \\(a\\le b\\) lexicographically.</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n  \\[\n  a_{ij}=a+e_i-e_j,\\qquad b_{ij}=b-e_i+e_j.\n  \\]</p><p>For distinct first two labels, include\n\\[\n\\sum_{i\\ne j}\\mathcal M(\\mathbf s;(a_{ij},b_{ij},c))=0\n\\]\nusing\n\\[\n\\mathbf s=(0,1,2),(0,1,0),(0,1,1)\n\\]\nwhen \\(c\\ne0\\), and \\(\\mathbf s=(0,1,0)\\) when \\(c=0\\). Include it only if all \\(36\\) shifted triples have total order at most eleven.</p><p>For equal first two labels, fix \\(i\\), sum over all six \\(j\\), and subtract\n\\[\n\\mathcal M((0,u);(-c,c)).\n\\]\nUse \\((0,0,u)\\) with \\(u=0,1\\) when \\(c\\ne0\\), and \\(u=0\\) when \\(c=0\\). Include both shift directions and retain each equation only if all six shifted triples have total order at most eleven.</p><p>These equations are precisely (36)–(37) after applying the reductions. Add the pair equations recorded in `rows0`. Register all coordinates encountered, including those that cancel in an individual equation.</p><p>Sort coordinates by\n\\[\n(\\mathbf 1_{\\mathbf s\\ne\\varnothing},\\operatorname{wt}(A),(\\mathbf s,A)),\n\\]\nthen apply the exact priority list `I` in Appendix A, Listing 1, lines \\(332\\)–\\(370\\). The program obtains the table dimensions in the statement.</p><p>Apply Proposition 1 to the retained integer witness rows. The exact denominator-cleared checks establish every proposed substitution for real moment vectors.</p><p>No completeness of this finite equation system is required. A vanishing expression encountered later may still be set to zero by its independently proved vanishing rule, whether or not its pair expansion strengthened this table.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.4", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Every \\(Z_\\lambda\\) is positive definite.", "statement": "<p>**Exact positivity of the six certificate matrices — original Section 7.4.**</p><p>Definitions and assumptions:</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(Z_\\lambda\\) be the six symmetric integer matrices defined from the literal certificate records in Proposition 7.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Every \\(Z_\\lambda\\) is positive definite. In the exact Schur-complement checks, all \\(98\\) successive pivots are rational numbers greater than \\(40000\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>The routine `makeZ` checks that the listed lower triangle has exactly \\(m(m+1)/2\\) entries and reconstructs the symmetric matrix.</p><p>The routine `posdef` uses rational arithmetic. At each step write the remaining matrix as\n\\[\nB=\n\\begin{pmatrix}\nC&amp;u\\\\\nu^{\\mathsf T}&amp;p\n\\end{pmatrix},\n\\]\ncheck \\(p&gt;40000\\), and replace \\(B\\) by\n\\[\nC-\\frac{uu^{\\mathsf T}}p.\n\\]\nFor complex \\(x,t\\),\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix}x\\\\t\\end{pmatrix}^*\nB\n\\begin{pmatrix}x\\\\t\\end{pmatrix}\n=\nx^*\\left(C-\\frac{uu^{\\mathsf T}}p\\right)x\n+\np\\left|t+\\frac{u^{\\mathsf T}x}{p}\\right|^2.\n\\]\nPositive pivots therefore prove positive definiteness inductively.</p><p>The exact checks on the six literal matrices give the asserted \\(98\\) pivots.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.5", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "There is a finite expansion \\( \\mathcal Q=c_0+\\sum_{K\\ne\\varnothing}c_K\\mu_K, \\) where each \\(\\mu_K\\) is one of the original nonconstant pair or mixed moments, and …", "statement": "<p>**Exact residual expansion of the positive forms — original equations (41), (42), and (46).**</p><p>Definitions and assumptions:</p><p class=\"li\">Assume the setting and fixed data of Proposition 7.</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal Q=\n  5\\sum_\\lambda10^{15-e_\\lambda}\n  \\mathbb E(Y_\\lambda^*Z_\\lambda Y_\\lambda).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Use the reductions in Lemma 7.2 and the exact substitutions of Lemma 7.3.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a finite expansion\n\\[\n\\mathcal Q=c_0+\\sum_{K\\ne\\varnothing}c_K\\mu_K,\n\\tag{46}\n\\]\nwhere each \\(\\mu_K\\) is one of the original nonconstant pair or mixed moments, and\n\\[\nc_0=-\\frac{613302797399911}{6480},\n\\qquad\n\\sum_{K\\ne\\varnothing}|c_K|\n=\n\\frac{179721388988719}{6480}.\n\\]\nNo new linear combination of moments is assigned an absolute-value bound.</p>", "proof": "<p>Expand each \\(Y_\\lambda\\) by its signed permutation sum. The action convention is\n\\[\n(P_pP_qv)_i=v_{q(p(i))},\n\\]\nimplemented by `perm(perm(v,q),p)`.</p><p>Replace every real Gram entry by Lemma 7.1 using charge triple\n\\[\n(a,-v,v-a).\n\\]\nAfter multiplying the entire form by five:</p><p class=\"li\">an entry not involving two \\(F_1\\) functions has weight five;</p><p class=\"li\">an entry involving two \\(F_1\\) functions has weights one and four for equal and distinct external labels.</p><p>These are exactly the weights assigned by `product`.</p><p>Because \\(Z_\\lambda\\) is real symmetric, summing diagonal entries and twice the lower off-diagonal entries gives\n\\[\n\\mathbb E(Y_\\lambda^*Z_\\lambda Y_\\lambda).\n\\]\nThe routine `gaps` uses this lower-triangle convention and retains the factor \\(10^{15-e_\\lambda}\\). Its expression is therefore exactly \\(\\mathcal Q\\), including for complex-valued \\(Y_\\lambda\\).</p><p>Apply `evmix`, then the certified mixed substitutions, and combine equal moment coordinates. The Appendix A calculation checks exactly\n\\[\n6480c_0=-613302797399911,\n\\]\n\\[\n6480\\sum_{K\\ne\\varnothing}|c_K|=179721388988719.\n\\]\nIt also checks that their sum is negative.</p><p>The complete expected verifier output is\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{l}\n\\texttt{rank table: 8871 909 897}\\\\\n\\texttt{rank table: 46041 3429 2767}\\\\\n\\texttt{-613302797399911/6480\\quad179721388988719/6480\\quad5000000000000000}.\n\\end{array}\n\\]\nThe final entry is the overall scale \\(5\\cdot10^{15}\\).</p><p>Every retained coordinate remains an original moment:\n\\[\nm(A)=\\text{a real average of unit-modulus monomials},\n\\]\nor\n\\[\n\\mathcal M(\\mathbf s;\\mathbf a)\n=\n\\text{a real average of products of character sums}.\n\\]\nThe substitutions create expressions in these coordinates, not new bounded coordinates.</p><p>The exact computation is the standard-library Python verifier printed in Appendix A, with assertions enabled. The paper gives its SHA-256 checksum as\n\\[\n\\texttt{104a7306205b8544c8f913f6afdb59603f2e04a207c9ac592c383b80eb0e2aa9}.\n\\]\nAll equality and inequality checks certifying the result are integer or rational checks; modular arithmetic is used only to propose substitutions subsequently checked exactly.</p>"}], "edges": [[0, 2, "cite"], [0, 14, "cite"], [3, 7, "cite"], [3, 19, "cite"], [4, 0, "cite"], [4, 1, "cite"], [4, 16, "cite"], [5, 7, "cite"], [6, 7, "cite"], [6, 18, "cite"], [7, 4, "cite"], [7, 19, "cite"], [8, 0, "cite"], [8, 2, "cite"], [8, 13, "cite"], [8, 14, "cite"], [9, 8, "cite"], [9, 10, "cite"], [10, 5, "cite"], [10, 8, "cite"], [11, 0, "cite"], [11, 2, "cite"], [11, 13, "cite"], [11, 14, "cite"], [12, 13, "cite"], [12, 18, "cite"], [13, 1, "cite"], [14, 13, "cite"], [14, 15, "cite"], [15, 13, "cite"], [15, 18, "cite"], [16, 1, "cite"], [17, 3, "cite"], [17, 21, "cite"], [18, 19, "cite"], [18, 21, "cite"], [19, 21, "cite"], [20, 16, "cite"], [21, 16, "cite"]]});
